          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Eilenberg-Watts theorem
            identifies colimit-preserving
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+modules"} categories of modules
            with the operations of forming
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+modules"} tensor products
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodules"} bimodules
          %h2#statement Statement
          %h3#for_ordinary_rings_and_modules For ordinary rings and modules
          .num_theorem
            %p
              %b Eilenberg-Watts Theorem
            %p
              Given unital
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              and an
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodule"} bimodule
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+modules"} tensor product
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi R
                    %mi N
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (-) \otimes_R N
                    \\;\colon\;
                    Mod_R \to Mod_S
            %p
              between the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+modules"} categories of modules
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+functor"} additive
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocontinuous+functor"} cocontinuous
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon Mod_R \to Mod_S
              is additive and cocontinuous, then it is naturally isomorphic to tensoring with a bimodule.
          %p
            This theorem was more or less simultaneously proved in (
            %a{:href => "#Watts"}> Watts
            ) and (
            %a{:href => "#Eilenberg"}> Eilenberg
            ).
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Given an additive cocontinuous functor
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msub
                        %mi Mod
                        %mi R
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi Mod
                        %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon Mod_R \to Mod_S
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              = succeed "-bimodule" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the underlying right
                  = succeed "-module" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi S
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi R
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(R)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                  is regarded as a right module over itself in the canonical way;
              %li
                %p
                  the left
                  = succeed "-module" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi R
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(R)
                  is given for
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi r
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \in R
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in N
                  by
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi r
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≔
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi r
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi n
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ,
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        r \cdot n \coloneqq F(r\cdot(-))n
                        \\,,
                %p
                  where
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi r
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi R
                        %mo →
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \cdot (-) \colon R \to R
                  denotes the right
                  = succeed "-module" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi r
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              The theorem holds for nonunital rings as well, but then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              reconstructs as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(R_1)
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_1
              is the extension of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              by adjoining the unit element (the tensor product is still over the original
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+functor"} flat functor
              then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(R_1)
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+module"} flat module
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p There are various equivalent ways to state the hypotheses of the theorem:
            %ul
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                is additive and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocontinuous+functor"} cocontinuous
                (i.e. preserves
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                is additive,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+exact+functor"} right exact
                and preserves
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                is additive and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
          %p
            The theorem is stated in the last form, for instance, in (
            %a{:href => "#Hovey"}> Hovey, Theorem 0.1
            ).
          %p
            In fact both bimodules and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intertwiners"} intertwiners
            as well as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            form a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              two
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi S
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi Func
                      %mi coc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi R
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    {}_R Mod_{S} \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} Func_{coc}(Mod_R, Mod_S)
            %p
              from the category of bimodules to that of colimit-preserving additive functors between their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+modules"} categories of modules
              is an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
          %h3#for_enriched_categories For enriched categories
          %p
            Rings can be seen as one-object
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %mi Ab
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = Ab
            is the category of abelian groups made symmetric monoidal with the usual tensor product of abelian groups. Similarly, bimodules between rings are the same as
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"} profunctors
            between one-object
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is the category of
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            Suppose that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Benabou+cosmos"} Benabou cosmos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V Mod
            where:
          %ul
            %li
              objects are small
              = succeed "-enriched" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %li
              morphisms are
              = succeed "-enriched" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %li
              2-morphisms are
              = succeed "-enriched" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p
            There is also a symmetric monoidal bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mi Cocont
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V Cocont
            where:
          %ul
            %li
              objects are the
              = succeed "-enriched" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [C^{op},V]
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              ranges over all small
              = succeed "-enriched" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %li
              morphisms are cocontinuous
              = succeed "-functors," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              = succeed "-functors" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              = succeed "-enriched" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %li
              2-morphisms are
              = succeed "-enriched" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              = succeed "-functors." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %p Then the following is surely true, though a reference would be helpful:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#generalized_eilenbergwatts_theorem Generalized Eilenberg-Watts’ Theorem
            %p
              Given a Benabou cosmos
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mi Prof
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V Prof
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mi Cocont
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V Cocont
              are equivalent.
          %h3#for_other_internal_monoids_and_internal_modules For other internal monoids and internal modules
          %p
            The standard Eilenberg-Watts theorem is a statement about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
            and their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/actions"} actions
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Hovey"} Hovey
          %h3#ForInfinityAlgebrasAndInfinityModules
            For
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            In particular Eilenberg-Watts theorems hold true in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            following
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            (see at
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+modules+over+an+algebra+over+an+operad"} model structure on modules over an algebra over an operad
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} model structure on topological spaces
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} model structure on simplicial sets
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+chain+complexes"} model structure on chain complexes
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+spectra"} model structure on spectra
                (symmetric, orthogonal,
                = succeed "-modules)." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝕊
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{S}
          %p
            This is the main theorem in (
            %a{:href => "#Hovey"}> Hovey
            ).
          %p
            More generally, we have the first half of the Eilenberg-Watts theorem in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{C}, \otimes)
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} monoidal (∞,1)-category
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+objects+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} simplicial objects in an (∞,1)-category
              such that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
              preserves this in each variable, then for all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} A-∞ algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B,C
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-bimodules"} ∞-bimodules
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi B
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (-) \otimes_B (-) \;\colon\; {}_A Mod_{B} \times {}_B Mod_{C} \to {}_{A} Mod_{C}
            %p
              preserves
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-colimits"} (∞,1)-colimits
              separately in each argument.
          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#Lurie"}> Lurie, cor. 4.3.5.15
            ).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalence
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+functor"}> additive functor
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tor"} Tor
          %h2#References References
          %p The original articles are
          %ul#Watts
            %li
              Charles E. Watts,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Intrinsic characterizations of some additive functors
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 11
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://mathscinet.ams.org/mathscinet/article?mr=118757"} MR118757
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.2307/2032707"} doi
          %ul#Eilenberg
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Eilenberg"}> Samuel Eilenberg
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Abstract description of some basic functors
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 24
              %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=0125148"} MR0125148
          %p A generalized statement in which the codomain is not assumed to be a category of modules is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              A. Nyman, S. Paul Smith,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A generalization of Watts’s Theorem: Right exact functors on module categories
              = succeed ":7" do
                %strong 44
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0806.0832"} arxiv/0806.0832
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1080/00927872.2015.1065873"} doi
          %p
            Generalization to
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher algebra
            is discussed in
          %ul#Hovey
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mark+Hovey"}> Mark Hovey
              ,
              %em The Eilenberg-Watts theorem in homotopical algebra
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0910.3842.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p and
          %ul#Lurie
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Algebra"} Higher Algebra
